# Online Network Database Manager

This file is technical only. It does not touch on the theoretical experience that agents have.

The database component the server owns: the data on disk and in memory, as one
thing rather than two. It is ours, not an embedded engine. Three premises shape
the format.

## The kernel never crashes

A `write(2)` that returns has its bytes in the page cache, and the kernel will
write them out. A process that dies and is restarted sees them. So *process-crash
durability needs no `fsync`* and no direct-I/O dance — the syscall returning is
enough.

What this does not remove is the process dying at an arbitrary instruction, so
the format has to tolerate a *torn tail*: a record half-written when the
process was killed. That is the one crash case that remains, and it is a
formatting problem rather than a durability one.

## Corruption is local

A corrupted unit must not take the rest with it. This decides the format more
than the other two premises:

- *Frame every unit* — length-prefixed or fixed-size — so a unit's extent is
  knowable without reading what precedes it.
- *Checksum every unit*, header and payload. Without it, corruption is silent
  wrongness; with it, it is a detected miss that can be skipped.
- *No whole-file index.* If finding anything requires one global block, then one
  bad block loses the store. Chunk the index and make it rebuildable from the
  data, or replicate it.
- *Append-mostly, and truncate the tail on open.* The torn last record is the
  common case, not the exotic one, so recovery is dropping it and carrying on.
- *Recover per unit, not per open.* Opening skips a *damaged* unit and reports
  it, so one bad block never makes everything unusable — which is exactly the
  non-local impact being ruled out. Damage is the one case this tolerates; an
  intact unit this build cannot decode aborts the open (*The type model, and the
  check at open*).

The pairing that makes this cheap: no `fsync` and per-unit checksums together
give up the guarantee between writes and get it back per record.

## Compaction happens at the edges

On-disk data is compacted when the server starts and when it stops — never in the
middle.

Between those edges the writer only appends, so a running server has a single
writer and nothing to coordinate. A compaction in the middle would move units that
readers are using, and that is exactly where a fault stops being local: the same
bytes would mean two things depending on when they were read. At an edge there is
no one to confuse — the server is starting, or it is finished.

## Persistent and in-memory are one thing

The in-memory structure *is* the model and the file is a rendering of it, so the
format question is how to write that structure down and read it back, not how to
design a schema on disk. Reads are pointer-chases; durability is an output.

## One file per running prompt, forked with it

This was written before the world model was fixed as global and unique as one
(`research/agent-world.dj`). If there is a single record, a fork cannot be a copy
of a whole file belonging to one prompt, and the question below is what has to be
re-answered rather than the layout being chosen from it.

A candidate layout: one file per running prompt, and a fork *copies* it, so the fork's file
is its parent's as of that moment and the two diverge independently afterwards.

It fits everything above — each file has a single writer, compaction stays at the
edges, and a fork costs a copy rather than a rewrite. It also gives the fork
something the turn model wants: the new running prompt begins with its parent's turns in
place, not with a reference it has to chase on every read.

What it does not give is the other direction. Running prompts have to be able to reference
each other's turns (`research/turns.dj`), and a copy is a snapshot — a reference
into the original's *later* turns cannot be answered from the fork's copy. So
either a reference always resolves against the file it names, whichever file the
reader forked from, or the fork is not a copy at all but a shared file that
separates only when written.

## Schema evolution and mutability

Two requirements that rule out the simple shapes.

*Every record carries its own version.* A turn's shape follows the tool set, so a
record has to say what it is — a tag and a version — and a reader decodes the tags
it knows and refuses an open that holds one it does not. Nothing is decoded by position
and no reader assumes a fixed layout, or every schema change becomes a migration of
every record instead of a new tag. This is what keeps evolution cheap in the one
direction that is safe: an old record stays readable as it is, because the reader
keeps a decoder for its version, and only derived data (an index, a cache) is ever
rebuilt. It is not a licence to write a version no reader knows — an unknown tag
aborts the open, so a new tag ships with the reader that decodes it.

*Turns are mutable*, so the file is append-mostly rather than write-once.
Rewriting a variable-length record in place is where locality of corruption is
lost: a partial rewrite leaves a record that is neither the old value nor the new
one, and no checksum can say which half survived. An update therefore writes a
*new* payload elsewhere and then publishes it with a single store, so a reader
sees either the old record or the new one and never a mixture. The old payload is
garbage until the edge — which is the second reason compaction lives at the edges
rather than mid-life, and the reason it must not run while readers hold pointers.

## Protobuf

The record-shape problem above — a record that says what it is, so a reader
decodes what it knows and can tell what it does not — is what Protocol Buffers
already solves, and its wire format is worth copying whether or not the library
is used. <https://protobuf.dev>

*A message is a series of key-value records.* Each record is a field number, a
wire type, and a payload, and the tag encodes the first two as
`(field_number << 3) | wire_type` in a varint. The low three bits say how to find
the payload's end; the rest say which field it is. There are six wire types, four
of them in use — `VARINT` (integers, bools, enums), `I64` and `I32` (fixed-width),
and `LEN` (strings, bytes, submessages, packed repeats).

*That is the whole of schema evolution.* Because the wire type carries the length,
a reader that has never heard of field 9 can still find its end exactly, so it
knows *that* it cannot read the record rather than guessing wrong — and an
unknown field aborts the open, which is the safe reading of not knowing. Nothing
is decoded by position, and nesting costs nothing extra because submessages are
`LEN` records recursively.

What the format insists on, from its own best-practices page:

- Never re-use a tag number, and reserve the numbers of deleted fields.
- Never change a field's type, and never add a required field.
- Add enum values rather than changing them; never change a field's default.
- Repeated-to-scalar loses data; scalar-to-repeated does not.
- Binary for interchange, text format for humans only.
- Never rely on serialization stability across builds: the same message can
  serialize to different bytes, so those bytes are not a key.

Limits worth knowing: a serialized message must be under 2 GiB, and unmarshaling
depth is capped — 100 in Java and C++, 10000 in Go, with a plan to reduce it.

What protobuf gives us is the *record*, not the store. It has no notion of
mutation, no checksum, no framing of records into a file, and no index — so the
per-unit checksums, the torn-tail truncation and the edge-only compaction above
are all outside it, and its own answer to mutation is "write a new message". Its
one piece of advice that maps onto this project directly is to keep API messages
and storage messages apart, which is the same split as abstract and raw turns
(`research/turns.dj`).

Where it is worth having, and where it is not:

- *The seam, yes.* The socket between client and server carries turn
  modifications (`research/linux-process.dj`), which is exactly a versioned,
  language-neutral binary protocol, and `Any` carries an arbitrary message with a
  type URL for the part whose shape is not known in advance. The sibling project
  draws the line in the same place: `omp2` keeps protobuf in
  `crates/proto/proto/omp/*` and in the RPC-side crates (`envd`, `driver`,
  `gateway`, `inference`, `collab`), while `crates/dom`, its session tree,
  serializes with JSON — protobuf between components, a readable format for the
  model.
- *The store, no.* Everything the sections above ask of the store is outside
  protobuf — no checksum, no framing of records into a file, no index, and no
  mutation.
- *The payload, no.* A tool set that changes while the agent runs cannot be a
  compiled message, so a record's body is a tag plus opaque bytes, and protobuf
  degrades into the container around them, discarding the typing it was chosen
  for. The type section below reaches the same conclusion from the other end: the
  schema is the Zig type, and there is no `.proto` to drift from the code.

Practically, `protoc` is installed on this machine, and every Zig implementation
is a dependency — nothing here declares one.

*Redaction is not a serialization problem.* `research/agent-world.dj` requires
that a redaction destroys: the old value has to be gone rather than superseded,
and the destruction has to reach every fork that copied it. No encoding does that,
and protobuf has two properties pushing the wrong way — unknown fields are
preserved in binary serialization, so a value can survive a round trip through a
layer whose schema never knew it, and serialization is eager while the history is
append-only, so the bytes remain in every earlier record and every copy. "Gone"
has to be engineered at the store level, by destroying a key the ciphertext
depends on or by rewriting the units; a format can only fail to help.

## What the kernel already provides

A file is an abstraction, and the thing underneath it here is the page cache.
`mmap` with `MAP_SHARED` is the interface to it: the mapped region *is* memory,
stores to it are loads and stores, and the kernel is the writer. Given that the
kernel never crashes, a store that has happened is durable — no `msync`, no
`fsync`, no write syscalls. That is as close to a persistent in-memory data
structure as Linux gets without hardware.

What else is on the table, and what this machine actually has:

- *tmpfs, `shm_open`, `memfd_create`* — a file object that is memory and can be
  shared, but not persistent: it dies with the last reference or the reboot.
  `/tmp` and `/run` here are tmpfs, so a runtime-directory file is one of these,
  and durable data cannot live there.
- *Persistent memory (DAX)* — `mmap` on a pmem device gives load/store access with
  no page cache, and `MAP_SYNC` makes a store durable without `msync`. This
  machine has none: no `/dev/pmem*`, no `nd` bus.
- *hugetlbfs and `MADV_HUGEPAGE`* — larger pages for the mapping. It buys TLB
  reach, not persistence. No hugetlbfs is mounted; tmpfs here is `huge=advise`.
- *Pinned eBPF maps* (`BPF_F_MMAPABLE` on bpffs) — a kernel-owned structure that
  outlives the process and can be mapped. Real, but fixed-size arrays only rather
  than a general store, and bpffs is not mounted here.
- *A raw block device* — you would own the layout and lose the page cache, which
  is the part doing the work.
- *`FICLONE` (reflink)* — not a store, but the root filesystem here is btrfs, so
  copying a file is O(1) and diverges copy-on-write. That is exactly the cheap
  fork the section above was looking for.

So the shape to build on is `MAP_SHARED` on a btrfs file: pointer-addressable,
kernel-written, and cheap to fork. Two consequences follow. The kernel offers no
per-unit integrity, so "corruption is local" is entirely our own checksums over
our own layout. And a record is written by a sequence of stores rather than one
syscall, so a torn tail is a sequence that stopped — which the checksum and the
truncate-on-open already cover.

## The type model, and the check at open

Well-typed is a property of the model, not of the encoding. JSON, Ion and the
protobuf wire format above all say what bytes look like; none says what a value
*is*. A schema does — a closed set of record types, each with declared fields,
ranges and references, and a decoder that accepts a value only when it fits one.
Schemaless JSON accepts anything; Ion is typed at the scalar level (timestamp,
decimal, blob, symbol); protobuf is typed at the field level but its schema lives
in a `.proto` beside the code. None of the three is strict on its own, so
"stricter" is not a fourth format: it is an application schema, and it is a
decision separate from which encoding carries the bytes.

That schema is the Zig type. The in-memory structure is the model, so the model
is a closed set of tagged unions — a record is a tag, a version and a payload —
and those declarations *are* the schema. There is no IDL and no `.proto` to drift
from the code, which is the pattern `src/omp_features.zig` already uses for the
feature vocabulary and `src/json_encoder.zig` for driving an encoding from
per-type declarations rather than a method per type. A new tag is a new variant;
a reader decodes the tags it knows and aborts the open on one it does not, which
is the one piece of the protobuf section worth keeping whatever carries the
bytes.

The check runs at open, and it is the load. Decoding every unit into the type is
what builds the in-memory model from the file, so there is no separate pass to
forget: open = drop the torn tail = decode every unit = the model. What open does
with a unit it cannot read splits on the checksum, the only thing that tells
*damage* from *difference*:

- *The checksum fails.* The unit is damaged: it is skipped and reported, and the
  open continues (*Corruption is local*).
- *The checksum holds, but the tag, the version, or the payload's declared type
  is one this build does not know.* The unit is intact and this build is simply
  the wrong one for it. The open *aborts*. Skipping here would be worse than
  failing: the value is real, and the next thing a running server does with a
  store it only half-read is compact it — deleting exactly what it skipped.

What this covers is the structure, not the words. Tags, versions, ids, references
and offsets are typed and checked; the payload of a tool result or a message is
bytes by nature, typed as exactly that and opaque inside. Strictness is over the
shape a record has, not over what a tool put in it.

A type error therefore cannot reach the middle of a run. Writes emit typed values
because writes are code, not untrusted input, so a type error on disk has only
two sources: corruption, which the per-unit checksum catches, and a schema
version, which the tag decides to decode or refuse. The open-time decode catches
both before the server serves, and every value it then holds passed that same
decode. The start edge is one pass, not two — drop the torn tail, decode every
unit into the model, then compact by writing the validated model back out — and
compaction there cannot reintroduce a type error, because it writes from the
model that just passed the decode.

Two spines are on the table and they cannot both hold, and that is what decides
the encoding rather than the typing question above:

- *The mapped region is the memory image* (*What the kernel already provides*).
  The bytes *are* the typed values, so there is no decode and no serialization —
  and therefore no JSON, Ion or protobuf as the store, only as an export. The
  open-time check is then a structural scan: every discriminant a known tag,
  every offset in range, every checksum valid.
- *The file is a serialization decoded at open* (*Persistent and in-memory are
  one thing*, and the protobuf section). Then the bytes are a text or protobuf
  rendering and the check above is a decode into the Zig types — a decode step
  and two representations rather than one.

The doc's own rule from the protobuf section — binary for interchange, text for
humans only — already rules out JSON or Ion as the store under either spine; as
an export format they are unconstrained. Which spine this is decides everything
downstream and is not yet decided.

## What this holds

The turns and their state (`research/turns.dj`) are the payload, so the durable
form of a turn is this component's question rather than the turn model's.

It is the *abstract* turn that is stored. How an abstract turn renders into a
*raw* turn is the turn model's business and not this component's: the store holds
the shape, and the rendering is a derivation from it that happens elsewhere.
